        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340582",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${h}^{′}(x)$ if $h(x)=ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(csc⁡x)+(ln⁡(x))(-csc⁡(x)cot⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{csc⁡(x)}{x}-ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x)cot⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
